---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { doc, DOCS_PR } from '../config';

const conditions = [
  ['Device', 'iPad Air 11-inch (M3), iPadOS 27.0, Developer Mode on'],
  ['App', 'DeviceProbe, MacBridge’s research app: a development build installed with Apple’s standard developer tools'],
  ['Signing', 'Every library signed by the owner’s own Apple development team. The development signature includes get-task-allow, which distribution builds do not have.'],
  ['Code', 'Original NORD LABS C and Objective-C test code. The same source built for iPadOS and for macOS, ARM64 both. No Apple or Blender code.'],
  ['Repetition', 'Each group of measurements run twice, with identical results.'],
];

const steps = [
  ['Code signature registered with the system; library validation', 'Accepted', 'Accepted'],
  ['Mapped, pointers rebased, imports bound', '0 bytes differ', '0 bytes differ'],
  ['Start-up code (initializer) ran', 'Yes', 'Yes'],
  ['Exported functions called · expected 42, 30, 6, 8, 1', '42 · 30 · 6 · 8 · 1', '42 · 30 · 6 · 8 · 1'],
];

const values = [
  ['42', 'only if the start-up code ran, through a bound import'],
  ['30', 'only if a pointer was rebased'],
  ['6', 'only if an import was bound'],
  ['8', 'only if a lazily bound import works'],
  ['1', 'only if zero-filled memory and the previous call were right'],
];

const notShown = [
  ['A standalone macOS program', 'The test code was a library called by the app. A program needs an entry point, a process environment, arguments and an exit.'],
  ['Arbitrary macOS application execution', 'Only tiny, purpose-built NORD LABS fixtures have run. No other developer’s code.'],
  ['An AppKit application', 'No windows, no AppKit. Objective-C ran only for two small classes of MacBridge’s own.'],
  ['Blender, at any stage', 'Blender is thousands of times larger, signed by its own developer, and needs Python, files, threads and much more.'],
  ['Code signed by another developer', 'Every library shared the app’s developer team. Whether another team’s signature is accepted is untested.'],
  ['App Store or TestFlight builds', 'Only a development build was tested. Distribution signing may behave differently.'],
  ['Any way around a platform protection', 'None was attempted. The code passed the system’s own signature and library-validation checks.'],
];
---

<Base title="First Contact" description="On 2026-10-10, MacBridge's experimental loader ran its own ARM64 macOS-built C and Objective-C test libraries on a physical iPad Air (M3). What was measured, under which conditions, and what it does not show.">
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Research milestone · 2026-10-10"
    title="First Contact"
    lede="For the first time, code built as a Mac library executed on a physical iPad through MacBridge. It was tiny, it was MacBridge’s own, and it ran under development signing. Those conditions matter as much as the result."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-amber/30 bg-amber/[0.05] p-5 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ti md:max-w-3xl">
      <strong class="text-paper font-semibold">A narrow result.</strong> No macOS application, no standalone macOS
      program, no AppKit application and no Blender code has run on an iPad. What ran were small original
      test libraries written by NORD LABS.
    </div>
  </PageHero>

  <!-- plain language -->
  <section class="border-b hairline">
    <div class="wrap grid gap-10 py-20 md:grid-cols-[1fr_1.5fr] md:py-24">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">In plain language</p>
        <h2 class="h2 mt-4">Refused by one loader. Run by another.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed text-ti">
        <p>
          A Mac library is ARM64 code plus a description of what it needs. The iPad’s own loader reads that
          description, sees “made for macOS”, and refuses the file before any of it runs. That refusal was
          measured again on the iPad for this experiment.
        </p>
        <p>
          MacBridge’s experimental loader does the preparation work macOS’s loader normally does. Given a small
          test library built for macOS, it asked the iPad to register and check the library’s code signature,
          placed the code in memory, connected every pointer and import, ran the start-up code, and called its
          functions. They returned exactly the values they were designed to return.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- conditions -->
  <section class="border-b hairline">
    <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-24">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal">The conditions</p>
      <dl class="reveal mt-8 divide-y divide-white/[0.07] border-y hairline">
        {conditions.map(([k, v]) => (
          <div class="grid gap-2 py-5 md:grid-cols-[12rem_1fr] md:gap-8">
            <dt class="font-mono text-[0.72rem] tracking-[0.12em] uppercase text-ti-2 md:pt-0.5">{k}</dt>
            <dd class="leading-relaxed text-paper/90">{v}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- measured -->
  <section class="border-b hairline">
    <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-24">
      <div class="reveal max-w-2xl">
        <p class="eyebrow">What was measured</p>
        <h2 class="h2 mt-4">The same source, built twice.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal table-wrap mt-10">
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>iPadOS-built</th><th>macOS-built</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Apple’s loader</td><td class="num">loaded</td><td class="num" style="color:var(--color-amber)">refused: incompatible platform</td></tr>
            {steps.map(([s, a, b]) => (
              <tr><td>{s}</td><td class="num">{a}</td><td class="num">{b}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal mt-16 grid gap-8">
        <div class="max-w-2xl">
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold">Why these numbers</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ti">Each value depends on specific loader steps, and all of them need the code mapped executable. If any step had been wrong, a specific number would have come out wrong.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border hairline bg-white/[0.07] grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-5">
          {values.map(([v, why]) => (
            <li class="bg-ink p-5">
              <span class="font-mono text-3xl text-ice">{v}</span>
              <p class="mt-3 text-[0.8rem] leading-snug text-ti-2">{why}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="reveal mt-16 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-7">
          <p class="eyebrow">ARM64 thread-local variables</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ti">A third library uses per-thread variables, set up through MacBridge’s own ARM64 entry code: <span class="text-paper">3 descriptors, 0 unexpected differences, self-check passed</span>. Its first run on Apple hardware; previously checked only under an emulator.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-7">
          <p class="eyebrow">Objective-C · same day</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ti">An own Objective-C library built for macOS: two classes registered through Apple’s documented runtime functions, then sent real messages. <span class="text-paper">Self-check 1, twice</span>; a deliberately broken variant failed as expected.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- not shown -->
  <section class="border-b hairline">
    <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-24">
      <div class="reveal max-w-2xl">
        <p class="eyebrow">The limits are part of the result</p>
        <h2 class="h2 mt-4">What this does not show</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="reveal mt-10 divide-y divide-white/[0.07] border-y hairline">
        {notShown.map(([t, d]) => (
          <li class="grid gap-2 py-5 md:grid-cols-[18rem_1fr] md:gap-8">
            <p class="flex items-start gap-3 text-paper"><span class="mt-[0.55rem] h-px w-3 shrink-0 bg-ti-2" aria-hidden="true"></span>{t}</p>
            <p class="leading-relaxed text-ti">{d}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal mt-10 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-ti">
        Before this run, the deciding question was whether MacBridge’s loader could work on an iPad at all,
        inside the platform’s rules. For MacBridge’s own, same-team-signed code under development signing, the
        answer is now yes, measured, in this configuration. How other configurations behave is unknown.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- evidence -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap py-20 md:py-24">
      <div class="reveal card flex flex-col gap-8 p-8 md:flex-row md:items-center md:justify-between md:p-10">
        <div class="max-w-xl">
          <p class="eyebrow">Public evidence</p>
          <p class="mt-4 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.015em]">Read the full First Contact report</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ti-2">
            Published in the MacBridge-Project documentation, currently in an{' '}
            <a href={DOCS_PR} class="text-ti underline decoration-white/20 underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper">open documentation pull request</a>.
            Raw logs stay in the private implementation repository.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a href={doc('docs/research/first-contact.md')} class="btn btn-primary">First Contact report <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a href={doc('docs/evidence.md')} class="btn btn-ghost">How results are established <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
